---
import { curlSnippet, fromSource, producerConfig } from '@pbcp/client';
import { parseCardOrThrow, toFrontMatter } from '@pbcp/core';
import { Marked } from 'marked';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { HEAD_REPO_URL, REPO_URL, href } from '../config';
import { connectors } from '../lib/connectors';
import { rfcSections } from '../lib/markdown';

const sec = rfcSections();
const rfc = (n: string) => `${href('/rfc/')}#${sec.get(n)}`;
// The same placeholders as the connectors' READMEs; the text below is what the packages produce.
const placeholders = { api: '<head url>', owner: '<owner>', token: '<token>' };
const config = JSON.stringify(producerConfig(placeholders), null, 2);
const curl = curlSnippet(placeholders);
const withSetup = connectors().filter((c) => c.setup);
const fromSourceHtml = new Marked({ gfm: true }).parse(fromSource()) as string;
// The reply loop of §11.1: the agent's decision card, and the record it posts after sending. Parsed, so they stay valid cards.
const mailCard = 'mail:work:3f2a9c0d1b7e4a65';
const ask = toFrontMatter(parseCardOrThrow({
  protocol: 'pbcp/1', id: 'hermes:gmail:19a4f2e7c3b1', kind: 'decision', to_person: 'morten', producer: 'hermes',
  thread: 'gmail:19a4f2e7c3b1', urgency: 'brief', title: 'Reply to Anna re invoice timing',
  summary: 'Anna asks to pay the September invoice on the 20th instead of the 15th. I propose agreeing for September only.',
  actions: ['approve', 'reject', 'edit'], references: [mailCard], emitted_at: '2026-10-07T06:00:00Z',
  body: 'Anna (Nordic Supply) asks whether the September invoice can be paid on the 20th instead of the 15th.\n\n## Proposed reply\n\n> Hi Anna, the 20th is fine for September. Let\'s keep the 15th from October on.',
}));
const sent = toFrontMatter(parseCardOrThrow({
  protocol: 'pbcp/1', id: 'hermes:gmail:19a4f2e7c3b1:sent', kind: 'artifact', to_person: 'morten', producer: 'hermes',
  thread: 'gmail:19a4f2e7c3b1', urgency: 'log', title: 'Sent: Re: invoice timing',
  summary: 'Sent the approved reply to Anna: the 20th is fine for September.',
  references: ['hermes:gmail:19a4f2e7c3b1'], emitted_at: '2026-10-07T08:11:20Z',
  body: 'Sent to Anna (Nordic Supply) as approved.',
}));
---
<Base title="Producers" path="/producers/" description="What a producer needs to reach a head, a first card with curl, and where each connector's setup is.">
  <h1>Producers</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A producer is anything that sends cards: an agent, a script, a mail reader. It needs three values from the head,
    and an HTTP client.
  </p>

  <h2>Three values</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The head URL.</strong> Where the head serves the producer operations: its <code>api</code>
      (<a href={rfc('7.5')}>§7.5</a>), or else the URL its description is read from. <code>GET</code> on the origin
      returns the description.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The owner.</strong> The person the head stores cards for, the <code>owner</code> in its description. Every
      card is addressed to it in <code>to_person</code> (<a href={rfc('10')}>§10</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A token.</strong> Issued by the head and bound to one namespace root: every id the producer sends starts
      with that root (<a href={rfc('12')}>§12</a>). How a head issues tokens is up to the head; the
      <a href={HEAD_REPO_URL}>reference head</a> mints them on its settings page, one per producer, shown once and
      revocable.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>As the environment the repository's connectors read, from <code>producerConfig</code> in <code>@pbcp/client</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>{config}</code></pre>

  <h2>A first card</h2>
  <p>
    Submit is a <code>PUT</code> to the card's id (<a href={rfc('7.1')}>§7.1</a>). This sends the example card from
    <a href={rfc('3')}>§3</a>, addressed to the owner. Its id is under the root <code>hermes</code>, so it needs a token
    bound to that root. It is the output of <code>curlSnippet</code> in <code>@pbcp/client</code>, which takes any other
    card too.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{curl}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The head answers <code>201</code> with the stored card, now carrying the fields it owns (<code>state</code>,
    <code>change_seq</code>, <code>received_at</code>, <code>delivery</code>). The same command again answers
    <code>200</code> and changes nothing: the id names the card (<a href={rfc('5')}>§5</a>).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>That answer is the confirmation.</strong> A <code>201</code> or <code>200</code> with the stored card means
    it landed; afterwards it also appears in <code>GET /changes?after=</code> with the same token. There is no producer
    read of a single card: <code>GET /cards/&#123;id&#125;</code> is not part of the protocol.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the decision</h2>
  <p>
    The person decides in the head. A producer reads decisions with <code>GET /changes?after=</code>
    (<a href={rfc('7.2')}>§7.2</a>): every card in its namespace that changed after a cursor, in order. It keeps the
    returned <code>next</code> and passes it back, and acts on each decision in a way that is safe to repeat.
    <a href={`${REPO_URL}/tree/main/packages/client`}><code>@pbcp/client</code></a>'s <code>runProducer</code> is that
    loop with a durable cursor; <a href={`${REPO_URL}/tree/main/packages/mcp`}><code>@pbcp/mcp</code></a> gives an agent
    the same operations as tools.
  </p>

  <h2>A reply loop, end to end</h2>
  <p>
    Approving a drafted reply needs nothing beyond the core (<a href={rfc('11.1')}>§11.1</a>). A decision is the
    authorisation; the producer that asked acts on it. Orders are for instructions the person gives unprompted, never a
    consequence of a decision (<a href={rfc('4.3')}>§4.3</a>).
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>The agent reads the mailbox and forms the mail reader's card id the way the reader documents it (<a href={rfc('5')}>§5</a>).</li>
    <li>It submits a <code>decision</code> card under its own root: <code>references</code> names the mail card, <code>summary</code> states the proposal, the body ends in <code>## Proposed reply</code>, and the actions are <code>[approve, reject, edit]</code>.</li>
    <li>The person decides in the head.</li>
    <li>The agent reads the decision from <code>GET /changes</code>. On <code>approve</code> it sends the reply, on <code>edited</code> it sends <code>edited_proposal</code>, and on <code>reject</code> or <code>dismissed</code> it sends nothing.</li>
    <li>After sending, it posts a <code>log</code> artifact, <code>Sent: &lt;subject&gt;</code>, that <code>references</code> the decision card.</li>
    <li>The mail card is the reader's. Deciding it with its default, <code>archive</code>, files the message.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The two cards the agent sends, in the file form:</p>
  <pre><code>{ask}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{sent}</code></pre>

  <h2>Kind and urgency</h2>
  <p>
    <code>kind</code> is what the card asks of the person (<a href={rfc('4.1a')}>§4.1a</a>): a <code>decision</code> to
    choose, with <code>actions</code>, open until decided; a <code>signal</code> to know that something happened; an
    <code>artifact</code> to read something made for them. <code>urgency</code> is when: <code>immediate</code>
    interrupts, <code>brief</code> waits for the next brief, <code>log</code> is recorded and never surfaced on its own
    (<a href={rfc('9')}>§9</a>). The two are independent.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A decision is <code>brief</code>, unless it cannot wait.</li>
    <li>A signal is <code>brief</code>, and <code>immediate</code> when it concerns the next few hours.</li>
    <li>
      An artifact is <code>brief</code> when it is read daily (a report, an agenda, a digest), and <code>log</code> when
      it is a routine record (a commit, a completed task, a producer connecting).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Until the packages are published</h2>
  <div set:html={fromSourceHtml} />
  <p>
    In a checkout, <code>@pbcp/mcp</code> runs as <code>node packages/mcp/dist/src/bin.js</code> in place of
    <code>npx -y @pbcp/mcp</code>. Each connector's Setup says how it runs from the checkout.
  </p>

  <h2>Connectors</h2>
  <p>
    Each connector's README has a Setup section with the three values in place and whatever else it needs. It is
    generated from the connector's own <code>connect()</code>, so it matches the code.
  </p>
  <ul class="links">
    {withSetup.map((c) => <li><a href={c.setup}><code>{c.dir}</code> setup</a> <span class="muted">— {c.settings.filter((s) => !s.startsWith('PBCP_')).slice(0, 4).join(', ') || 'no further settings'}</span></li>)}
  </ul>
  <p>The <a href={href('/connectors/')}>connectors page</a> lists every connector and its settings.</p>

  <h2>Next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={rfc('11.1')}>§11.1</a>, the producer checklist.</li>
    <li><a href={href('/rfc/')}>The RFC</a>, for threads, supersession and urgency.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
